      <!-- August 2026 — Blind Person's Allowance + owner-manager comparisons -->
      <article class="relative pl-8 border-l-2 border-primary/20">
        <div class="absolute -left-2 top-0 h-4 w-4 rounded-full bg-primary"></div>
        <div class="mb-2">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center rounded-full bg-primary/10 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-primary">
            August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Blind Person&rsquo;s Allowance corrected, and our sole trader vs limited company answer has changed</h2>
        <ul class="text-sm text-muted-foreground space-y-2">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500 shrink-0"></span>
            <span><strong class="text-foreground">Blind Person&rsquo;s Allowance is £3,250 for 2026/27</strong>, not the £3,130 we were using — that was last year&rsquo;s figure. The <a href="/calculator/blind-persons-allowance-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Blind Person&rsquo;s Allowance calculator</a> was also handing back only 20% of the allowance to higher-rate taxpayers, showing roughly half the real saving. A 40% taxpayer now correctly sees <strong class="text-foreground">£1,300</strong> a year, and a 45% taxpayer £1,462.50.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500 shrink-0"></span>
            <span><strong class="text-foreground">Incorporating no longer cuts your tax bill if you draw everything out.</strong> Our <a href="/calculator/sole-trader-vs-ltd-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">sole trader vs limited company</a> tools were charging a flat 25% Corporation Tax with no marginal relief and taxing every dividend at the basic 10.75%. With both corrected, the sole trader comes out ahead across £20,000–£100,000 of profit — by about £5,300 at £100,000. The case for a company now rests on liability protection, retaining profit and pension contributions rather than on tax alone.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500 shrink-0"></span>
            <span><strong class="text-foreground">Contractor day rates were coming out about 15% too low.</strong> The <a href="/calculator/contractor-day-rate-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">day rate calculator</a> assumed a flat 25% uplift over your target take-home instead of solving for it. To take home £60,000 over 220 days you need <strong class="text-foreground">£405</strong> a day, not the £345 we were quoting. The <a href="/calculator/ir35-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">IR35 calculator</a> now also deducts employer NI from the inside-IR35 side, which it had been leaving out.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500 shrink-0"></span>
            <span><strong class="text-foreground">Premium Bonds now pay 4.35%, and we were three numbers behind.</strong> NS&amp;I raised the prize fund rate from 3.80% to <strong class="text-foreground">4.35%</strong> and shortened the odds from 22,000 to <strong class="text-foreground">21,000 to 1</strong> for the September 2026 draw. Our <a href="/calculator/premium-bonds-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Premium Bonds calculator</a> was still on 3.80% while the surrounding text said 4.00%, so a £10,000 holding showed an expected £380 or £400 depending on where you looked. It is £435 now, and £50,000 expects £2,175 a year rather than £1,900.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500 shrink-0"></span>
            <span><strong class="text-foreground">Company car tax was a percentage point too high on most cars, and wrong on every plug-in hybrid.</strong> HMRC&rsquo;s table pauses at 21% across 70&ndash;79 g/km before resuming at 22%, and our <a href="/calculator/company-car-tax-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">company car tax calculator</a> stepped straight through it, so everything from 75 g/km up came out 1 point high. Separately, cars emitting 1&ndash;50 g/km are banded by <strong class="text-foreground">electric range</strong>, not CO2 &mdash; from 4% at 130+ miles to 16% under 30 miles. We were using a flat 5%. The calculator now asks for the electric range when it matters, and every band from 0 to 200 g/km matches HMRC&rsquo;s published table.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500 shrink-0"></span>
            <span><strong class="text-foreground">Retail, Hospitality and Leisure relief has ended, and importing a car costs more than we said.</strong> The 40% RHL business rates discount ran in 2025/26 and stopped on 31 March 2026; from April 2026 it is replaced by permanently lower RHL multipliers with no cash cap and nothing to apply for. Our <a href="/calculator/car-import-duty-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">car import duty calculator</a> was also charging 6.5% on non-EU imports &mdash; that is the motor caravan rate. Passenger cars are <strong class="text-foreground">10%</strong>. London&rsquo;s average Band D council tax is now £2,068, not £1,902.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500 shrink-0"></span>
            <span><strong class="text-foreground">The maintenance loan calculator was overstating the loan for higher household incomes.</strong> It treated £10,991 as the smallest London loan, but that is simply where the published gov.uk table stops, at a £45,000 household income. The loan keeps tapering below that to a basic rate of <strong class="text-foreground">£7,039</strong> in London, £5,048 away from home outside London and £4,013 living with parents. The <a href="/calculator/student-maintenance-loan-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">maintenance loan calculator</a> also used a straight line between the maximum and the minimum instead of the real withdrawal rate, so a London student on a £70,000 household income was shown £10,991 rather than £7,039. It now applies the actual taper &mdash; £1 of loan for every £6.36 to £6.54 of income above £25,000 &mdash; and reproduces every row of the official table exactly.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500 shrink-0"></span>
            <span><strong class="text-foreground">Our Building Regulations U-value summary mixed up two different Part L targets.</strong> The <a href="/calculator/insulation-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">insulation calculator</a> quoted 0.30 W/m&sup2;K for cavity wall insulation and 0.18 for a floor. Those are the standards for building a <em>new</em> element, such as an extension. When you upgrade something the house already has, Approved Document L asks for <strong class="text-foreground">0.55</strong> for a filled cavity wall and <strong class="text-foreground">0.25</strong> for a floor. The loft figure (0.16) and the replacement window figure (1.4, or a Window Energy Rating of Band B) were right and stay as they are &mdash; including under the 2026 edition of Approved Document L, which takes effect on 24 March 2027.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500 shrink-0"></span>
            <span><strong class="text-foreground">Emergency pension tax and tax refund estimates</strong> were starting the 45% rate £12,570 of income too early (48% in Scotland), overstating the bill by up to £628 — £377 for Scottish taxpayers. Both are corrected.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>

